  "Question": "At Frank’s Fruit Market, \\(3\\) bananas cost as much as \\(2\\) apples, and \\(6\\) apples cost as much as \\(4\\) oranges. How many oranges cost as much as \\(18\\) bananas?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(6\\)",
    "B": "\\(8\\)",
    "C": "\\(9\\)",
    "D": "\\(12\\)",
    "E": "\\(18\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Use apples to connect the two price comparisons. There are \\(18\\div3=6\\) groups of \\(3\\) bananas in \\(18\\) bananas. Since each group costs as much as \\(2\\) apples, \\(18\\) bananas cost as much as \\(6\\cdot2=12\\) apples.\n\nNow \\(12\\) apples are two groups of \\(6\\) apples. Each group costs as much as \\(4\\) oranges, so the same cost buys \\(2\\cdot4=8\\) oranges. The answer is \\(\\boxed{8}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
